e a whole crop production  based on this succession or this  particular cultivar you can see how  quickly you can lose  you know an entire crop just due to  climate conditions or other conditions  that we can't control  so really genetic diversity is  really lacking in the u.s tart cherry  industry and that's not for lack of  access to material so our tart cherry  collection comes primarily from michigan  state from uh dr amy ayazoni who was the  tart cherry and rootstock breeder there  so she did a lot of explorations  throughout europe to bring back a lot of  this material which is the the majority  of our collection so i've got some  show and tell here so this is a  hungarian variety  which ripens you know several weeks  earlier than even montmorency  in the united states but i mean you're  going to see  you know super this beautiful large  fruit and a delicious  um flavor as well  and then we still have  some of the accessions are you know  weeks behind so these still have a ways  to go in terms of fruit expansion and  ripening so each  each two trees is unique in our  collection again contrasted with an  orchard system where every cultivar  might be the same a kind of a  a model crop there but you can see just  the beautiful  crop load on on this particular  accession that's really weighing down  the branches  again it it looks ideal and maybe i get  we get a lot of criticism well why  aren't growers growing this well it it  might not match up to to what the  industry is looking for so mechanical  harvesting uniformity and just you know  whatever the the market really demands  so most of the tart cherry industry in  the us is dedicated towards you know  processing or you know some other  application not necessarily fresh eating  which i i hope will change because there  are some delicious tart cherries here  that i think would do well in the in a  fresh market  so one of the challenges of maintaining  a clonal diversity collection is of most  of our collections