 plays a really  large role in that effort to promote  sustainability so we're thinking fewer  pesticides easier maintenance easier  harvesting and those traits can be mined  from diversity collection like ours  and so typically this is our our largest  and most popular collection and we we do  receive a lot of requests for materials  so it's a free service um that we offer  to researchers and our commercial  partners um and they typically request  cyan material so that they can propagate  the the material for them for their own  but we also do fruit requests and people  characterize or they request pollen for  their own breeding purposes but we are a  collection that's not only here and  maintained but utilized and that's you  know the most important aspect i think  of our of our effort i mean what's  what's the point of keeping things if  breeders or researchers aren't going to  use them  and so we typically you know fulfill  about 500 requests and send out about 5  000 units from our collection annually  and that that number just seems to be  going and that higher and higher and  that's a an excellent indication of our  value to our stakeholders so the apple  collection is unique in that we have one  of the best backup systems in cryo  using cryopreservation and so we do lose  a lot of our  trees just primarily due to fire blight  and so having that backup and i know  typically one of the points i always try  to make is that cryo preservation isn't  just end of the world scenarios we  heavily rely annually on receiving that  material from backup i mean part of the  the challenge of maintaining diversity  collection is dealing with diversity of  susceptibility and and vulnerability to  these diseases or environmental  conditions and so  it's about 60 to 70 percent backed up in  cryo preservation so it is a little bit  early in the season so it is you know  there is tremendous diversity and just  the the the way that the trees look and  the their shapes and you can