ous which  maybe again at this early stage isn't as  recognizable but this is one of the most  important cultivars in modern breeding  programs so  you know pedigrees and genetic studies  really show us that  modern programs really have relied  heavily on about 15 accessions or 15  cultivars  in apple improvement which is kind of  scary right like we look at the full  diversity of apple and here we've been  using a really you know narrow genetic  base  for improvement and i we do see that  changing a lot with introgression of  wild species and um again genomic  selection and genomic resources really  do help us better leverage wild and more  diverse apple genetic resources  this one again does have a a unique  story when it was first developed by  starks brothers they put it in a cage  you know they found this novel tree that  they saw potential in and they didn't  want anyone else stealing it so they  erected a steel cage around it so people  couldn't steal  um the germplasm and that really i mean  as breeders and as this group right we  we really do you know want to safeguard  what we have right and but we have a  kind of an opposite mission to really  share this material with the world we  want more people to have access to our  genetic diversity so that breeders can  make their advance you know selections  this has been a production of cornell  university on the web at cornell.edu

