 are maintained in the  field which means they are susceptible  to disease to the elements um  we lose lots of branches just due to  heavy winds that hit the collection on  these two particular  you know trees are really struggling  with the disease that makes them already  weak and vulnerable  and the challenge is again like the only  way really to maintain the diversity is  to to clonally propagate it  and so tart cherries don't do very well  in cryo preservation where we can store  bud material in liquid nitrogen kind of  in a safe  you know out of the environmental  conditions  and so that that really does make us  rely more heavily on these field  you know collections which again there's  really no easy way to replace some of  these cultivars if they're lost or to  disease or to the environment  so we mentioned that that tart cherry is  a tetraploid hybrid so this is the  european dwarf cherry  prunus fruiticosa  you can see a very distinctive dwarf  habit small fruit and  you know  yeah not all that desirable fruit  quality you can actually go back to the  the sweet cherry and do a tart cherry  sweet cherry hybrid and that's becomes a  duke cherry  so this is a hungarian land race  that dr iazoni brought back  and just amazing fruit tart cherry has a  really distinctive soft fruit texture in  contrast to the sweet cherry  i prefer it a lot of  you know my colleagues just don't and so  the taste preference for for tart cherry  or for cherries in general it tends to  be on the more crunchy side in north  america  certainly um  with the growing and the you know kind  of  world view and all the pallets that were  you know exposed to in the the u.s  market certainly we can  i think there's a place for more  diversity and crop and flavors and  textures and hopefully in the future  so really you need to be in here you  need to come visit the collection  because it's really difficult to talk  about easier for you to just walk  through and see and this really is kind  of mind-boggling when we're used to  you know  horticultural or  orchard systems where uniformity is is  paramount right we want  precision in agriculture we're kind of  dealing with all of the genetics some  sometimes it's kind of messy so in terms  of things bloom a different time the  spray application disease resistance  it's it's a challenge for us  all right so we are standing in the  united states department of agriculture  apple diversity collection and it is  really a world premiere collection one  of the largest and most genetically  diverse  of its kind so  um we typically have about five thousand  to six thousand seedlings or trees on  site and we also have a large seed  storage of malice wild species in a seed  vault um preserved at minus 20 celsius  but again the challenge with with clonal  conservation is that we do need to keep  trees in the field for evaluation for  access  to the germplasm so really as a curator  we we really do need to balance  accessibility and vulnerability of the  genetic resources um so we are standing  here here in a  the what we call the core diversity  collection of the apple which is 250  distinct accessions that represent the  diversity of the entire apple collection  so here we have lots of common or  popular cultivars some developed here at  cornell including empire and john of  gold  and then we have a lot of wild material  and so  one of the big things that when students  or visitors come to see us they they  look at some of these small fruited  trees and they say what's what's the  value  well apple is is unique in that all  species are  you know you can do inter-crossing or  inter-hybridization  to bring in some of the the interesting  or novel traits  of the wild relatives into a  domesticated background and that is  really important when you start thinking  about disease resistance or  climate adaptations and it's the overall  goal right is to increase  sustainability of our crop production  and that does genetics plays a really  large role in that effort to promote  sustainability so we're thinking fewer  pesticides easier maintenance easier  harvesting and those traits can be mined  from diversity collection like ours  and so typically this is our our largest  and most popular collection and we we do  receive a lot of requests for materials  so it's a free service um that we offer  to researchers and our commercial  partners um and they typically request  cyan material so that they can propagate  the the material for them for their own  but we also do fruit requests and people  characterize or they request pollen for  their own breeding purposes but we are a  collection that's not only here and  maintained but utilized and that's you  know the most important aspect i think  of our of our effort i mean what's  what's the point of keeping things if  breeders or researchers aren't going to  use them  and so we typically you know fulfill  about 500 requests and send out about 5  000 units from our collection annually  and that that number just seems to be  going and that higher and higher and  that's a an excellent indication of our  value to our stakeholders so the apple  collection is unique in that we have one  of the best backup systems in cryo  using cryopreservation and so we do lose  a lot of our  trees just primarily due to fire blight  and so having that backup and i know  typically one of the points i always try  to make is that cryo preservation isn't  just end of the world scenarios we  heavily rely annually on receiving that  material from backup i mean part of the  the challenge of maintaining diversity  collection is dealing with diversity of  susceptibility and and vulnerability to  these diseases or environmental  conditions and so  it's about 60 to 70 percent backed up in  cryo preservation so it is a little bit  early in the season so it is you know  there is tremendous diversity and just  the the the way that the trees look and  the their shapes and you can see  some of the indications of fruit but  here is one that's  particularly interesting this is called  robert's crab and this has a novel  red flesh to it i mean this you can't  even see any white by the end of the  season it's just pure red but again i  think novelty and horticultural breeding  is is the future  and so your these new  varieties that come out have to compete  with you know not just other apples but  you know all fruits from all over the  world as we you know the markets are  kind of flooded with more exotic fruits  and so here's an apple that has you know  not only a beautiful red deep red on the  outside  but it accumulates anthocyanin on the  inside so kind of a novel eating  experience  this is robert's crab really starchy  kind of a terrible flavor but i think  within a few generations of breeding and  certain cultivars have come out red  flesh that are you know much better in  terms of dessert or eating quality so  during the the initial founding of the  collection in 1983 the the collection  really was heavily focused on acquiring  cultivars and pre-breeding material and  then we saw this transition over to  collecting crop wild relatives and so  one of the most you know important crop  wild what relatives is the progenitor  species malice ceversii and so phil  forslyn the the first apple curator um  organized an exploration with herb  aldwinkle from cornell university to go  to kazakhstan to collect apples from the  the origin and so apple really is a  unique  crop in the u.s because we do we claim  it as our own but really it's quite an  exotic fruit so its origin was from  kazakhstan  and it was traded through the the silk  route into europe we're hybridized with  another species mal silvestris a  european crab apple so with malice  severity typically you'll see  a little bit larger fruit similar to the  cultivated apple  but it's lacking that acidic punch that  you really that consumers really want  so that's where so it's got sweetness  and size  and then the european malasavi has that  high acidity and so you combine the two  and you get this beautiful  you know  contrasting flavors  and so from there we get this  hybridization effect that led to what we  know as the modern domesticated apple  and certainly there has been some  selection  for for fruit size and sweetness and  acidity but really um apple in its in  its roots and its genetic diversity is  still quite untamed and so there's a lot  of potential that we still find in a lot  of  allelic and genetic diversity that's  still really untapped looking at the  pool of domesticated cultivars as well  as the crop wild relatives so we've  learned a lot about apple domestication  and its um evolution by looking at its  progenitor malice cersei and so we have  one of the one of the elite collections  premier collections of malice versi in  the world and kind of a  coming full circle was uh just over the  recent years collaborators from  kazakhstan have been requesting the  material to come back to their country  so we have a repatriation  act or activities that we're trying to  do to help them reintroduce their  heritage apple there  so some of it is um you know so some of  the diversity of mouse of rci is  endangered in its in its origin so a lot  of deforestation or other activities  causes loss of that genetic diversity  another challenge for wild apple is um  is admixture is that hybridization or  cross-pollination with cultivated apple  so a lot of the times we we go out and  explore for wild apples only to find  that they've already been hybridized by  by commercial or cultivated blocks which  is a challenge we want we want as pure  wild as we can so we can understand the  the  you know the unique attributes of a  particular species  so our effort yeah largely to support  our kazakh colleagues is to help them  reclaim their the material and  reintroduce um kind of prevent the  any future loss of malice of rci  we're standing at golden delicious which  maybe again at this early stage isn't as  recognizable but this is one of the most  important cultivars in modern breeding  programs so  you know pedigrees and genetic studies  really show us that  modern programs really have relied  heavily on about 15 accessions or 15  cultivars  in apple improvement which is kind of  scary right like we look at the full  diversity of apple and here we've been  using a really you know narrow genetic  base  for improvement and i we do see that  changing a lot with introgression of  wild species and um again genomic  selection and genomic resources really  do help us better leverage wild and more  diverse apple genetic resources  this one again does have a a unique  story when it was first developed by  starks brothers they put it in a cage  you know they found this novel tree that  they saw potential in and they didn't  want anyone else stealing it so they  erected a steel cage around it so people  couldn't steal  um the germplasm and that really i mean  as breeders and as this group right we  we really do you know want to safeguard  what we have right and but we have a  kind of an opposite mission to really  share this material with the world we  want more people to have access to our  genetic diversity so that breeders can  make their advance you know selections  this has been a production of cornell  university on the web at cornell.edu


